  "sections": [
    {
      "body": "The scoped evidence frames graphics as a field where mathematical structure, hardware pipelines, perceptual limits, and visual abstraction are coordinated to make complex scenes intelligible in real time. Shadercoding presents the most direct version of this frame: a shader is described as GPU-directed rendering logic, and fragment shaders can turn pixel coordinates, time, and inputs into animated mathematical images. The more applied sources extend the same frame to virtual crowds, foveated VR, edge bundling, and large graph drawings, where image-space or perceptual reductions help sustain interactivity instead of rendering every possible detail at uniform fidelity.",
      "citation_anchor_ids": [
        "anchor-shadercoding",
        "anchor-crowd-rendering",
        "anchor-foveated-rendering",
        "anchor-large-graph-lod"
      ],
      "claim_ids": [
        "claim-a-shader-is-a-gpu-directed-program-that-describes-rendering-processes-for-turnin--64c2250afac1",
