        "source-05-interactive-level-of-detail-rendering-of-large-graphs--df4eb719b5f7"
      ]
    },
    {
      "body": "Several themes recur across the corpus. First, GPU and image-space computation repeatedly act as enabling media: raymarching reconstructs apparent 3D depth inside fragment shaders, CUDA accelerates skeleton-based edge bundling, and OpenGL accumulation fields support graph level of detail without extra hierarchy storage. Second, abstraction is productive rather than merely reductive: impostors stand in for dense animated crowds, skeletons steer bundles through local structure, and foveated rendering intentionally varies quality by visual region. Third, constraints can be generative: the shadercoding evidence explicitly treats mathematical and technical limits as creative motors, while the system papers turn memory, latency, and overdraw constraints into design rules.",
      "citation_anchor_ids": [
        "anchor-shadercoding",
        "anchor-crowd-rendering",
        "anchor-edge-bundling",
        "anchor-foveated-rendering",
        "anchor-large-graph-lod"
      ],
