      ]
    },
    {
      "body": "The clearest open agenda comes from foveated rendering, where future work is described as needing broader human-visual features, improved gaze and AI techniques, better handling of new data types, more suitable rendering paradigms, stronger objective quality metrics, and deeper AR and task-oriented applications. The graph-visualization sources also leave open how users should calibrate the tradeoff between overview legibility and individual-edge faithfulness, especially when bundling overdraws edges or level-of-detail aggregation hides inner-cluster connections. Across the subspace, a larger question is how transferable the demonstrated successes are: shader performance, virtual-city crowds, skeleton-bundled graphs, foveated displays, and large graph renderers each show strong local solutions, but the context does not yet provide a unified evaluation standard for comparing visual quality, interaction cost, and information loss across domains.",
